course: everyday
lesson: 26
title: Wild animals: keep away, never feed
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Wild animals are not pets: even a calm-looking bear, alligator, kangaroo or fox can bite, scratch or charge if it feels cornered, surprised or protective of its young.
explain: The two golden rules are keep your distance and never feed wild animals: an animal that gets used to human food loses its fear of people, comes looking for more and may get aggressive, and often has to be put down.
explain: In bear country such as Alaska, Canada and Colorado, hikers stay in groups, talk or make noise so bears hear them coming, keep food locked away, and never run from a bear, but back away slowly while talking calmly.
explain: In Florida, alligators live in lakes, ponds and canals, so children stay well back from the water's edge, swim only in safe marked places in daylight and keep pets away from the water.
explain: In Australia, kangaroos can kick and scratch hard, and in spring magpies swoop to guard their nests: cover your head, walk quickly away from the nest area, and get off and walk your bike.
explain: In Britain and Ireland, foxes and badgers live close to people; they are best watched quietly from a distance, never fed by hand, touched or cornered.
explain: Campers in Canada left food in their tent; a bear came in the night to get it, and the park rangers later had to put the bear down because it had learnt to look for food near people.
explain: A boy in Florida went close to a pond's edge to look at a small alligator; a bigger one lunged out of the water, and he only just jumped back in time.
fact: Never feed wild animals; food from people makes them lose their fear and can make them dangerous.
fact: A mother animal protecting her babies is especially dangerous.
fact: Never run from a bear; back away slowly and talk calmly.
fact: Making noise on a hike helps bears hear you coming and move away.
fact: Food, rubbish and even toothpaste must be stored away from bears at a campsite.
fact: Alligators live in fresh water all over Florida, even in small ponds and canals.
fact: It is against the law to feed wild alligators in Florida.
fact: Alligators can lunge very fast over a short distance, in the water and on land.
fact: Male kangaroos can kick and scratch very hard, so they should be watched from a distance.
fact: Australian magpies swoop people in spring to protect their nests.
fact: Foxes and badgers may bite if they are cornered or touched.
fact: Moose in Alaska and Canada are huge and can charge and kick, so they can be dangerous, especially mothers with calves.
fact: The best way to enjoy wild animals is to watch them quietly from far away.
fact: Wild animals that are fed by people lose their fear and become more dangerous, and often have to be killed.
fact: Wild animals with babies are most likely to attack.
exercise: Why should you never feed a wild animal? | It loses its fear of people and can become dangerous | It will stop being able to find water for itself in the wild | Human food makes animals grow too big | Wild animals cannot taste our food
exercise: You see a black bear on a trail in Colorado. What should you do? | Back away slowly while talking calmly | Turn and run as fast as you can down the trail | Walk up to take a photo | Throw your sandwich to it
exercise: Why do hikers in bear country talk loudly or sing? | So bears hear them coming and move away | To make the bears come closer so they can see them | Because bears like music | To scare the birds away
exercise: At a campsite in Alaska, where should food be kept? | Locked away from bears, never in the tent | Inside the tent near your pillow, so you can guard it | On a plate outside the tent | In your sleeping bag
exercise: Where do alligators live in Florida? | In lakes, ponds, rivers and canals | Only in zoos | Only in the sea | Only in the far south of the state
exercise: Why should children stay back from the edge of a Florida pond? | An alligator could be hiding in the water | The water is always too cold for children to touch | Fish might jump out at them | The grass there is slippery
exercise: When is a wild animal often most dangerous? | When it is protecting its babies | When it is far away across a field | When it is moving slowly away from you | When it has not noticed you at all
exercise: A big male kangaroo stands up tall near a picnic in Australia. What should you do? | Keep away and do not approach it | Offer it some bread from your plate | Walk up quietly and pat it on the head | Stand in front of it to block its way
exercise: Why do magpies swoop on people in an Australian spring? | They are protecting their nests | They want to steal shiny bike helmets | They are hunting people for food | They are playing a game of chase with you
exercise: A magpie swoops while you are riding to school in Brisbane. What helps? | Get off, walk your bike and cover your head | Pedal straight towards the nest to scare it | Throw sticks at the magpie | Stop and wave your arms at it until it gives up
exercise: A fox comes into a garden in London every night. What is best? | Watch it quietly from the window | Hand-feed it every night so it becomes tame | Try to stroke it when it comes close | Chase it out with a broom every night
exercise: Which is the best way to enjoy wild animals? | Watch them quietly from far away | Get as close as you can, so they get used to you | Feed them so they come back | Pick them up gently
exercise: Why is a moose in Canada dangerous to walk near? | It is huge and can charge and kick, especially a mother | It eats people | It can spit poison | It climbs trees to jump on people
exercise: What happens to many bears that get used to human food? | They become dangerous and are often put down | They become gentle pets that live happily near camps | They learn to cook | They stop eating forever
test: Lucas camps with his family in Banff, Canada. He wants to keep his crisps in the tent overnight. Why is this unwise? | The smell can bring a bear to the tent | Crisps go soggy in the cold mountain air overnight | Squirrels eat tents | Crisps are not allowed in Canada
test: A girl in Florida sees an alligator lying still on a canal bank. Which is safest? | Stay far back and tell an adult | Walk past it closely, it is asleep | Throw it some bread to keep it happy | Poke it with a stick to see if it is alive
test: Which is true? | A fed wild animal can become dangerous to people | Feeding wild animals always helps them get through winter | Wild animals are just like pets | Only big animals can hurt people
test: A family in Alaska meets a bear close up on a trail. What should they do? | Stay together, back away slowly and talk calmly | Split up and run in different directions so it gets confused | Climb the nearest small tree at once | Shout and charge straight at the bear
test: A boy in Perth gets swooped by a magpie on his way to school each spring. Which plan is cleverest? | Take another way round the nest for a few weeks | Climb the tree to take the nest down so it goes away for good | Throw stones at the bird every day | Wave his arms wildly when it comes
test: A badger is trapped in a garden fence in Yorkshire. What should Ella do? | Stay away and ask an adult to call a wildlife rescue | Pull it out carefully with her bare hands | Feed it until it is calm | Try to pick it up in a towel and carry it home to look after
test: Why should a dog be kept away from the water's edge in Florida? | An alligator could grab it | Dogs cannot swim in fresh water | The water is bad for their fur | Fish might nip at its paws in the water
test: A wild kangaroo hops into a campsite looking for food. A child holds out some bread. What could go wrong? | The kangaroo could kick or scratch, and it learns to beg | Nothing, kangaroos at campsites are tame and like children | The bread will make it fly | The kangaroo will take the child home
test: Why is it wrong to walk between a mother moose and her calf? | The mother will defend her calf and may charge | The calf will follow you home and lose its mother | Moose are scared of children | The mother will lose her way
test: deer's so cute, can i give it my sandwich | No, never feed wild animals | Yes, a little bit is fine, it's hungry | Yes, if it comes close | Only the crusts
test: there's a bear!! run?? | No, back away slowly and talk calmly | Yes, run as fast as you can, bears are slow | Climb the nearest tree | Throw food at it
test: duck pond in florida, pond's fine to play by right | Careful, gators can live in small ponds | Yes, gators only live in the sea | Yes, ponds are too small for them | Yes, feed them if you see one
test: moose with a baby by the road, get closer for a pic? | No, mums with calves are dangerous | Yes, moose are gentle, they only eat plants | Yes, if you're quiet | Yes, give the calf some bread
test: there's a deer with a baby fawn right by the trail, can i go close | No, mothers protect their babies and can attack | Yes, deer are always gentle and never hurt anyone | Yes, if you go slowly | Yes, if you bring food
test: we've got leftover sandwiches, chuck em to the bear? | Never, a fed bear becomes dangerous and gets put down | Yes, it will be grateful | Yes, then it will leave you and the tents alone | Yes, if you throw them far
test: Josh says gators on land are slow and harmless. true? | No, they can lunge very fast over short distances | Yes, they are only fast in water, never on land | Yes, they never come out on land | Yes, unless they are hungry
test: my mum's mate said a bear came into the campsite and ate everyone's food cos someone left toothpaste in the tent | Real lesson: store food and even toothpaste away from bears; the detail may be exaggerated | Real lesson: bears only like toothpaste | It proves camping is never safe anywhere | It means bears are scared of tents
test: someone at school reckons alligators only live in swamps, so ponds in Florida are fine to swim in | Wrong; alligators live in fresh water all over Florida, even small ponds and canals | He is right, ponds are too small for them | He is right, they only live in the Everglades | He is right, alligators avoid places with houses
test: Kai got swooped by a magpie and cried, the year 6s keep calling him Bird Boy | Do not join in; magpies swoop in spring and anyone can get scared | Join in, the nickname is only a joke and he'll laugh about it later | Spread it, so he toughens up | Spread it, and say the bird chased him home
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the consequence of feeding wild animals? | The animals lose their fear of people and may become dangerous | The animals become permanently tame and safe | The animals are rendered healthier by human food | The animals cease to require any other food
exam: It being given that a bear is encountered at close quarters, select the course of action consistent with established guidance. | Not to run, but to withdraw slowly whilst speaking calmly | To run as quickly as possible downhill | To climb the nearest small tree at once | To approach the bear and offer it food
exam: Identify the principal reason for which hikers in bear country are advised to make noise. | So that bears hear their approach and move away | So that bears are attracted to observe them | So that other hikers may locate the bears | So that bears become accustomed to people
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning mother animals. | A mother protecting her young is especially dangerous | A mother with young is invariably calm and harmless | A mother animal will abandon her young if approached | A mother animal never defends her young
